<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The desktop suites' Process half (MOD8): the test classes that start real processes, run real ticks,
  or hold a wall-clock bound on either. They are the ones another worktree's load has failed (FLAKE1).
  The parent runs them in its full set, with this file as the test run's settings; a subagent's worktree
  runs the rest, filtered to Category!=Process. daoris.gates.json declares both halves of each suite, and
  each test project's Category.cs says which classes carry the trait, and why.

  The driver's git-only classes, marked Parallel=Git as well (PROC1b), are left to git.process.runsettings;
  a suite that marks none, as the modules' marks none, runs every Process class here. A test without the
  second trait is Parallel!=Git, as one without a category is Category!=Process.

  The driver's half runs eight classes at once (PROCPAR1): it ran one class at a time, 27 minutes of tests
  for 27 minutes of wall, though each class has its own home, scratch and ports. What a class shares with
  its neighbours (a variable of the process's environment it sets, the process's handles it measures, a
  bound of a few seconds on a real process) puts it in a collection that runs alone, after the rest: the
  driver's Alone.cs says which and why, and its CategoryTests holds the first two. xUnit's default
  algorithm starts a test only while fewer than eight run, an awaiting one included, so eight bounds the
  real processes too. The measurement behind eight is in D180's budget note.

  Whether a suite runs its classes at once is its assembly's to say, so this file says nothing of it: a
  ParallelizeTestCollections here would override the modules' Parallelism.cs, which runs that suite one
  class at a time because its modules read the process's environment. The driver's suite says nothing
  either, and xUnit's default runs its collections at once; CategoryTests holds this file to that.

  A file, not a run-settings argument at the end of the command line: VSTest ANDs this filter with any
  filter the caller adds, so the merge tool can re-run one failed test alone by adding its own. Whatever
  is appended after a trailing run-settings argument is read as one more: measured, that re-run built
  again and ran the whole half.
-->
<RunSettings>
  <RunConfiguration>
    <TestCaseFilter>Category=Process&amp;Parallel!=Git</TestCaseFilter>
  </RunConfiguration>
  <xUnit>
    <MaxParallelThreads>8</MaxParallelThreads>
  </xUnit>
</RunSettings>
